{
  "id": "COMPOUND:15365 - PROTEIN:3990",
  "verbalization": "Longistyline C is implicated in the regulation of the Inner centromere protein through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Longistyline C acts as a target for two distinct proteins, both of which are involved in the regulation of the Inner centromere protein. The first pathway involves UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2B17, which is targeted by 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid, and the second pathway involves Werner syndrome ATP-dependent helicase, which is targeted by Excisusin B. These two proteins are both involved in complex cellular processes that ultimately influence the function of the Inner centromere protein. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Longistyline C may modulate the activity of the Inner centromere protein, though the exact nature of this modulation remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a network of interactions that suggest a broader role for Longistyline C in cellular regulation, with the Inner centromere protein being a key downstream target.",
